    "title": "Cash vs Card",
    "blurb": "Understand what each one costs you.",
    "lead": "Cash and card are not identical products. One preserves your privacy, the other preserves your time.",
    "claim": "Cash is the more private option and the only one that many small traders can afford to accept without paying a card fee. Card payments are faster, more convenient and increasingly the only option in some places, and they are protected by strong consumer fraud rules — Chargeback and Section 75 give you a route to get money back when something goes wrong, which is why a blanket 'always use cash' rule can leave you worse off. The genuine costs to argue about are the card fees that small businesses pay and pass on, and the fact that digital payments make your spending easier to track. The UK has already legislated to end the fees that large chains charge for card acceptance, with the 'Project Darwin' work reducing them for smaller retailers.",
    "alternatives": "Use cash where you reasonably can and where the business prefers it, and keep a card for convenience and for the fraud protection. Set spending alerts on your account if tracking is a concern. Use a privacy-conscious card if you want less data attached to your spending.",
